 rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction uses \\(2n-2\\) rectangles and the lower bound shows that no\n   tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the permutation that records the\ncolumn of the unique uncovered unit square in each row:\nthe uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (any permutation works for the\nlower‑bound argument).  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has dimensions \\((n-k)\\times1\\).  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has dimensions \\((k-1)\\times1\\).\n\nThe rectangles are pairwise disjoint because each rectangle occupies a single\ncolumn and a set of rows that does not contain the hole of that column.\nAll unit squares except the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\) belong to exactly one of\nthe rectangles.  \nThe number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary\n\nConsider an arbitrary tiling satisfying the problem conditions and let\n\\(\\pi\\) be the associated permutation of holes.\n\nFor a fixed column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j\\) be the row of its hole, i.e. \\(\\pi(r_j)=j\\).\n\n*If \\(r_j\\neq 1\\) and \\(r_j\\neq n\\).*  \nThen column \\(j\\) contains cells both **above** the hole\n\\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<r_j\\}\\) and **below** the hole\n\\(\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j<i\\le n\\}\\).  \n