t \\(I_n=1\\), the total number of **increases** of the sequence \\((I_r)\\) must equal the total number of **decreases**. Moreover, each increase corresponds to a moment where a *new* rectangle appears for the first time (the rectangle that creates the new interval cannot have existed in the previous row, otherwise it would have contained the uncovered cell of that previous row). Hence the number of distinct rectangles that are first seen while scanning the rows from top to bottom is at least the number of increases.\n\nSince the sequence starts and ends with value\u202f1, the number of increases is exactly the number of times the value goes from 1 to 2, which can happen at most once per step. A simple counting argument shows that there must be at least \\(n-1\\) increases:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=1}^{n-1} (I_{r+1}-I_r)=I_n-I_1=0,\n\\]\nso the number of \\(+1\\) steps equals the number of \\(-1\\) steps.  \nBecause the value can never exceed 2, each \\(+1\\) step must be followed later by a \\(-1\\) step, and there are exactly \\(n-1\\) positions where a change (either \\(+1\\) or \\(-1\\)) can occur. Consequently there are at least \\(n-1\\) increases.\n\nThus **at least \\(n-1\\) distinct rectangles are needed** to account for the new intervals created while moving down the rows.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  The same argument for columns\n\nApply the same reasoning to the transpose of the board (swap rows and columns). Scanning the columns from left to right yields another set of at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles that are first seen during that scan. A rectangle that is first seen while scanning rows already has a well\u2011defined top side; when we later scan columns it will not be \u201cborn\u2019\u2019 again. Hence the two sets of rectangles are disjoint.\n\nConsequently the total number of rectangles in any admissible tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and Section\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Hence the minimum number of tiles Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
