e diagonal).  \nThus the \\(2n-2\\) tiles cover all cells except the diagonal holes, i.e. they satisfy the required condition.\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nArrange the rows in increasing order of the column of their hole:\n\n\\[\nh_{1}<h_{2}<\\dots <h_{n},\n\\qquad\\text{where }h_{k}= \\pi(r_{k})\\text{ and }r_{k}\\text{ is the }k\\text{‑th row in this order}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1 (Structure of a left‑side tile).  \nA tile that contains cells **only to the left** of the holes of the rows it occupies (a *left‑side tile*) has a column interval \\([L,R]\\) with the property  \n\n\\[\n\\forall\\; \\text{row }i\\text{ in the tile}\\;,\\; h_i>R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a row \\(i\\) belongs to the tile, the tile’s columns are a contiguous interval \\([L,R]\\).  \nSince the tile does not contain the hole \\((i,h_i)\\), the hole column must lie outside the interval, i.e. either \\(h_i<L\\) or \\(h_i>R\\).  \nBecause the tile is a left‑side tile, all its cells are left of the hole, so \\(h_i>R\\). ∎\n\nConsequently the set of rows that a left‑side tile can cover is a **suffix** of the ordered list \\((r_{1},\\dots ,r_{n})\\): if a row \\(r_k\\) belongs to the tile then every later row \\(r_{k'}\\;(k'>k)\\) also satisfies \\(h_{k'}>R\\) and may be included.  \n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2 (At least \\(n-1\\) left‑side tiles are needed).  \n\nConsider the rows \\(r_{1},r_{2},\\dots ,r_{n-1}\\).  \nRow \\(r_{k}\\) possesses left‑hand cells (columns \\(1,\\dots ,h_{k}-1\\)), because \\(h_{k}<h_{k+1}\\le n\\).  \nAny left‑side tile covering any of these cells must have rightmost column at most \\(h_{k}-1\\); by Lemma 1 such a tile can involve only rows whose hole column exceeds that rightmost column, i.e. rows from the set \\(\\{r_{k+1},\\dots ,r_{n}\\}\\).  \nThus the left‑hand cells of row \\(r_{k}\\) cannot b