overed unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nDenote by \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map\n\\(i\\mapsto \\pi(i)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is the permutation matrix\n\\(\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two monotone regions  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nU^{+}= \\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le n,\\; \\pi(r)<c\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nU^{-}= \\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le n,\\; 1\\le c<\\pi(r)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(U^{+}\\) consists of all cells **to the right** of the permutation path,\n\\(U^{-}\\) those **to the left** of it.  \nEvery cell not belonging to the permutation matrix lies either in\n\\(U^{+}\\) or in \\(U^{-}\\); the two sets are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nWe prove that any tiling of \\(U^{+}\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\nThe same argument applied to \\(U^{-}\\) yields another \\(n-1\\) rectangles,\nhence any tiling of the whole board needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*For the upper region.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\neq n\\) define the cell  \n\n\\[\na_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\in U^{+}.\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, one in each of the rows\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n**Claim.** No axis\u2011parallel rectangle can contain two distinct cells\n\\(a_i\\) and \\(a_j\\) (\\(i<j\\)) without also containing a hole.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).\nLet its column interval be \\([c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]\\) and its row interval\n\\([r_{\\min},r_{\\max}]\\).  \nBecause \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_j\\) belong to \\(R\\),\n\n\\[\nc_{\\min}