 of tiles  \n\nConsider the poset \\((U^{+},\\le )\\) where  \n\n\\[\n(r,c)\\le (r',c')\\iff r\\le r'\\ \\text{and}\\ c\\le c'.\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangle \\(R\\) is contained in \\(U^{+}\\), then any two cells of \\(R\\)\nsatisfy the above order; hence the set of cells of \\(R\\) is a **chain** in\nthe poset.  Consequently, covering \\(U^{+}\\) with rectangles is exactly a\nchain‑cover of the poset.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  A large antichain in \\(U^{+}\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\na_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\in U^{+}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then either \\(i<j\\) and \\(\\pi(i)+1\\le\\pi(j)\\) (because\n\\(\\pi\\) is a permutation) or the reverse; in either case we have\n\\(\\pi(i)+1\\not\\le\\pi(j)+1\\).  Hence no two distinct \\(a_i\\) are comparable,\nso \\(\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_{n-1}\\}\\) is an **antichain** of size \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Dilworth’s theorem\n\nDilworth’s theorem states that in any finite poset the minimum number of\nchains needed to cover all elements equals the size of a largest antichain.\nSince we have exhibited an antichain of size \\(n-1\\) in \\(U^{+}\\), any\nchain‑cover (hence any rectangle covering) of \\(U^{+}\\) requires at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  The lower region\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the symmetric antichain  \n\n\\[\nb_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1),\\qquad i=2,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nwhich lies in \\(U^{-}\\), shows that covering \\(U^{-}\\) also needs at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4  Global lower bound\n\nTherefore any admissible tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the uncovered\nsquares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\)).\n\n*Upper region.*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,c)\\mid i+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering all cells to the right of the\nhole \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Lower region.*  \nFor each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(r,j)\\mid j+1\\le r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nan \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) tile covering all cells below the hole \\((j,j)\\).\n\nThe rectangles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1},C_2,\\dots ,C_n\\) are pairwise\ndisjoint, none of them contains a diagonal cell, and together they cover\nevery unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes.  Their total number is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of Section 3 shows that no tiling can use fewer than\n\\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 4 uses exactly that many.\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the minimum number of tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
