ngle \\(T\\) that lies completely to the left of all holes it meets.  \nIf \\(T\\) occupies rows \\(r_1\\le r_2\\le\\cdots\\le r_m\\), then its column interval must be contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,\\dots ,\\min_{k}L_{r_k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the width of \\(T\\) cannot exceed the smallest \\(L_i\\) among the rows it covers.  \nWhen we pass from a row whose \\(L_i\\) equals \\(k\\) to a row whose \\(L_i\\) equals \\(k+1\\), the extra column \\(k+1\\) cannot be covered by the same rectangle; a new rectangle is required for that column.  \nHence each distinct positive value of \\(L_i\\) forces at least one separate rectangle.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells situated to the left of the holes.\n\nExactly the same reasoning applied to the right\u2011hand side, where the row\u2011lengths are \\(R_i=n-\\pi(i)\\), shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover the cells to the right of the holes.\n\nConsequently any tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s condition must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThus the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n**Left side.**  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{\\text{L}}=\\{(r,c)\\mid k+1\\le r\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k^{\\text{L}}\\) is a rectangle of height \\(n-k\\) and width \\(k\\).  \nIt lies entirely to the left of the diagonal, because in every row \\(r\\ge k+1\\) the diagonal column is \\(r\\ge k+1>k\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_k^{\\text{L}}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles, and their union is precisely the set of all cells with column \\(<\\) row (the region left of the diagonal).\n\n**Right side.**  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1